### Finding $\dfrac{dx}{dt}$

$\dfrac{dx}{dt}=\dfrac{2x}{y}$ [[☃ explanation 1]]

### Finding $\dfrac{dx}{dt}$ at $(2,9)$

The expression for $\dfrac{dx}{dt}$ depends on both the particle's $x$-coordinate $\blueD{2}$ and its $y$-coordinate $\maroonD{9}$:

$\begin{align}
\dfrac{dx}{dt}&=\dfrac{2(\blueD{2})}{(\maroonD{9})}
\\\\
&=\dfrac{4}{9}
\end{align}$
